# Summary
Introduced: the commit added cloud LLM calls that transmit unfiltered RAG context to external providers. The underlying retrieval query already lacked a visibility predicate, but the new cloud path creates a new external disclosure path and standalone `cloud_llm` mode without adding the required visibility filtering.
This commit adds `cloud_llm` as a standalone answer engine and as a cascade fallback, but it reuses the existing RAG matches without enforcing end-user visibility. The orchestrator query loads all active knowledge documents for the tenant and only selects `kd.visibility`; it does not exclude `agents_only`. The new `_build_context()` then concatenates every match above score threshold regardless of visibility, and `build_cloud_llm_answer()` sends that context to the configured cloud provider. A WhatsApp user can ask a question matching an internal/agents-only document; when `ANSWER_ENGINE=cloud_llm` is enabled, or when cascade falls back to cloud LLM after Ollama failure, those internal chunks may be sent to Anthropic/OpenAI and reflected in the bot response. This violates the documented RAG boundary that agent-only content must not be used for end-user replies, and it expands the leak to an external processor.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the application has a distinct `agents_only` visibility boundary for knowledge documents.
- [x] Confirm end-user RAG retrieval loads active tenant chunks without excluding `agents_only` documents.
- [x] Confirm ranking preserves and can return an `agents_only` match above LLM thresholds.
- [x] Confirm cloud LLM context construction/provider invocation includes above-threshold chunks without visibility filtering.
- [x] Confirm both standalone `ANSWER_ENGINE=cloud_llm` and cascade fallback paths pass unfiltered matches to the cloud LLM path.
## Report
Validated the finding. This is a Python information-disclosure bug, so a crash is not expected; direct PoC execution reproduced the disclosure with exit 0. Code review showed the boundary exists in schema: infra/postgres/01-schema.sql:288-306 defines visibility including 'agents_only'. The end-user RAG query in app/services/rag_orchestrator.py:287-297 selects kd.visibility but filters only tenant_id and kd.status='active'; there is no predicate excluding agents_only. rank_chunks() in app/services/rag_retrieval.py:119-128 preserves visibility on RetrievalMatch but does not filter it. The new cloud path builds provider context in app/services/cloud_llm_answer.py:35-44 by appending every above-threshold chunk_text with no visibility check, then passes that context to _call_provider at app/services/cloud_llm_answer.py:181 and 196-206. The standalone cloud engine path at app/services/rag_orchestrator.py:611-623 and cascade fallback at app/services/rag_orchestrator.py:678-694 pass matches/llm_candidates to build_cloud_llm_answer without filtering. Dynamic PoC command `PYTHONPATH=. python validation_poc_cloud_llm_visibility.py` created an agents_only chunk with secret text, ranked it using the real rank_chunks(), and monkeypatched only the external provider call. Output showed `"visibility": "agents_only"`, score 0.9029, provider `context_text` containing `CLAVE INTERNA ALFA-OMEGA...`, and `"direct_cloud_context_contains_agents_only_secret": true`. The same PoC exercised _resolve_answer() in standalone cloud_llm mode and cascade mode with simulated Ollama failure; both captured cloud calls had `"visibilities": ["agents_only"]` and `"contains_secret": true`, with logs showing `cascade.cloud_llm_attempt`. Debugger validation used `python -m pdb -c "break app/services/cloud_llm_answer.py:196" -c "continue" -c "p context" -c "where" -c "quit" validation_poc_cloud_llm_visibility.py`; pdb stopped immediately before `_call_provider(...)` and printed the local context containing the agents_only secret, proving the data reaches the cloud-provider call site. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable in the container (`valgrind_not_available`); gdb was also unavailable, and pdb was used as the appropriate Python debugger.

